# gitleaks allowlist — deliberate test fixtures, not credentials.
#
# Every entry below is an AWS-access-key-SHAPED STRING that story 005's secrets
# guard needs in order to be testable at all. `archive-story.sh` blocks an
# archive when gitleaks reports a finding (R1.3), and the only honest way to
# prove that is a fixture gitleaks genuinely detects. A fake key that the
# scanner ignores would turn those tests into tautologies.
#
# None of these is, or ever was, a real credential:
#   * they are AKIA-prefixed literals typed into bats fixtures;
#   * they authenticate against nothing;
#   * they live only in test files and in the story's authored-test copy.
#
# Fingerprints are pinned per commit:file:rule:line (history) and
# file:rule:line (working tree), so a NEW finding anywhere — including a real
# one in these same files — is still reported. This file suppresses exactly
# these occurrences and nothing else.
#
# WHICH DIALECT MATTERS TO WHOM. The two sections below are not interchangeable
# and are not redundant. History entries answer a repository-wide `gitleaks
# detect`; working-tree entries answer `gitleaks dir`, and they are the ONLY
# ones `archive-story.sh`'s secrets guard can use — it scans the working tree of
# one story directory. An entry filed in the wrong section is not a weaker
# suppression, it is no suppression at all.
#
# RUN BOTH FROM THE PROJECT ROOT. gitleaks builds each fingerprint's path by
# mirroring the source path AS GIVEN, so `gitleaks dir .` from here yields the
# root-relative form both this file and the guard use, while the same command
# run from a subdirectory — or with an absolute source — yields paths that match
# nothing. Story 009 exists because the guard itself passed an absolute path and
# could therefore never match a single line below.
#
# Regenerate after touching the secrets-guard fixtures, from the project root:
#   gitleaks detect --report-format json --report-path h.json
#   gitleaks dir . --report-format json --report-path w.json
#   jq -r '.[].Fingerprint' h.json w.json | sort -u
#
